        "side": "new",
        "severity": "medium",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "Driver-reported EEPROM length not bounded by fixed stack buffer",
        "explanation": "eeprom_info.length is set straight from module_info.eeprom_len, which the PMD reports, and eeprom_info.data points to a fixed stack array of EEPROM_DUMP_CHUNKSIZE bytes. rte_eth_dev_get_module_eeprom() checks only that data is non-NULL and length is non-zero; it never checks the caller's buffer size. If a driver reports an eeprom_len larger than EEPROM_DUMP_CHUNKSIZE, the driver writes past the end of bytes_eeprom and corrupts the stack. Common SFF lengths today (256, 512, 640) fit in 1024 bytes, so this takes an unusual or buggy PMD, or a larger module type such as CMIS, to trigger. The prior finding's second point, that eeprom_info is not zero-initialised, does not cause a problem: the get_module_eeprom path uses only data, offset and length, and all three are set.",
        "suggestion": "Bound the length before the read, e.g. `if (module_info.eeprom_len > sizeof(bytes_eeprom)) { fprintf(stderr, ...); continue; }`, or clamp it to sizeof(bytes_eeprom). Another option is to malloc a buffer of eeprom_len bytes.",
        "evidence": "eeprom_info.length = module_info.eeprom_len;",
        "confidence": 0.55,
        "id": "0e5dfd87a22d",
        "hunk_id": "app/proc-info/main.c#6",
        "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
        "tier": 3,
        "escalated_from": [
          1
        ],
        "also_reported_by": []
      },
      {
        "file": "app/proc-info/main.c",
        "start_line": 1615,
        "end_line": 1616,
        "side": "new",
        "severity": "medium",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "EEPROM length not bounded by fixed 1024-byte stack buffer",
        "explanation": "eeprom_info.length is taken from the driver-reported module_info.eeprom_len and the driver writes that many bytes into bytes_eeprom[EEPROM_DUMP_CHUNKSIZE]. If a PMD reports a length over 1024, the stack buffer overflows. Also, eeprom_info is not zero-initialized, so other fields such as the type field hold garbage.",
        "suggestion": "Clamp or reject when module_info.eeprom_len > sizeof(bytes_eeprom), and memset eeprom_info to 0 before use.",
        "evidence": "eeprom_info.length = module_info.eeprom_len;",
        "confidence": 0.5,
        "id": "a88bec73a596",
        "hunk_id": "app/proc-info/main.c#6",
        "model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
        "tier": 1,
