      {
        "file": "app/proc-info/main.c",
        "start_line": 1607,
        "end_line": 1623,
        "side": "new",
        "severity": "low",
        "category": "error-handling",
        "title": "Error on one port aborts the dump for all remaining ports",
        "explanation": "When rte_eth_dev_get_module_info() or rte_eth_dev_get_module_eeprom() fails for a port, the function returns. These calls commonly fail, for example with -ENOTSUP when the PMD has no support or with an error when no module is plugged in. So one port without a module stops the dump for every later port in the mask, even ports that have readable modules.",
        "suggestion": "Use `continue` instead of `return` in both error branches so the remaining ports are still dumped.",
        "evidence": "return;",
        "confidence": 0.5,
